{"name":"Gaboon Viper","hex":"#8c6450","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gaboon viper","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Gaboon viper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gaboon_viper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gaboon viper","blocks":[{"text":"The Gaboon viper, also called the Gaboon adder, is a large and highly venomous species of viper found in the rainforests and savannas of sub-Saharan Africa. It is the largest member of the genus Bitis. Like all other vipers, it is venomous, and it has the longest fangs of any venomous snake – up to 2 inches (5.1 cm) in length – and the highest venom yield of any snake. No subspecies are recognized."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Bitis_gabonica.jpg/330px-Bitis_gabonica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaboon_viper","wikidata":"Q371183"},{"title":"Viper","blocks":[{"text":"Vipers are snakes in the family Viperidae, found in most parts of the world, except for Antarctica, Australia, Hawaii, Madagascar, Ireland, and various other isolated islands. All vipers are venomous, and have long, hinged fangs that permit deep envenomation of their prey. Three subfamilies are currently recognized. They are also known as viperids. The name \"viper\" is derived from the Latin word vipera, -ae, also meaning viper, possibly from vivus (\"living\") and parere, referring to the trait viviparity common in vipers like most of the species of Boidae. The earliest known vipers are believed to have diverged from the rest of the clade Caenophidia in the early Eocene."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Viperidae-01.jpg/330px-Viperidae-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viper","wikidata":"Q163656"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia